WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.009
James Cameron's Avatar is the highest-grossing
film of all time, and it's not likely to lose

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:06.920
the title soon — if ever.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.130
Only Cameron's previous effort Titanic and
Star Wars: The Force Awakens have even come

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:16.230
within shouting distance of Avatar's staggering
nearly-$2.8 billion run.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.539
Yet there seems to be no rush getting the
obligatory Avatar sequels into theaters.

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:25.050
Last year, release dates were finally announced
for the next films in Cameron's planned series,

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.689
with Avatar 2 hitting screens on December
18, 2020.

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:32.710
But if that bit of news slipped by you, it
may be because the public's appetite for Avatar's

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:37.110
continuing story may not be as ravenous as
Cameron thinks — and because one massively

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.320
successful film, even the most successful
ever, may not necessarily translate to a sustainable

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.320
franchise.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.020
Here's why we're more than a little concerned
about Avatar 2.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.460
Memories fade

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.609
Extremely belated sequels can sometimes work,
but it's a tricky proposition.

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:57.890
Blade Runner 2049 is an illustration of how
it can go right creatively, but in general,

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.650
audiences are now accustomed to the big-budget
serialized storytelling of the Star Wars and

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.030
Marvel franchises.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:07.340
Attention spans are relatively short, and
fans usually have to wait no longer than a

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.110
year for the next installment to carry the
story forward in today's movie market.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:16.400
Avatar came out almost a decade ago, and when
the follow-up hits theaters in 2020, it'll

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.860
have been a full 11 years since the first
film stunned everyone into repeat showings.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.360
There's a chance that all the enthusiasm and
fan fervor that drove the first film's success

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.230
might not be as strong after so much time
away from Pandora.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.570
Climate change

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.610
The vast majority of the hype surrounding
Avatar in the months leading up to its release

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:39.410
had more to do with the technology involved
than the actual storyline or cast of characters.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.450
Avatar was promoted as the first movie to
treat 3D not as a gimmick, but as a serious

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.900
storytelling tool.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.270
As with Titanic before it, it was James Cameron's
revolutionary filmmaking techniques that had

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.220
audiences so intrigued by what he'd created.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.460
Avatar certainly delivered on its promise
of a bold new world of CGI and an immersive

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.990
3D film experience.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.580
But upon revisiting the picture through a
more current lens, the flaws of the film's

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:06.090
storytelling come into focus more than the
glowy space plants and Na'Vi costumes.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.550
For one thing, it's now widely agreed that
the film is derivative.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.230
Even James Cameron himself has admitted to
the central plot being an alien version of

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.590
Dances with Wolves.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.230
The film also leans into the same "white hero
complex" that's become so problematic and

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.890
it's been accused of ripping off everything
from animated films like Ferngully and Pocahontas

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.340
to Marvel Comics' Timespirits series.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.571
Even if none of the plagiarism accusations
levied against Cameron are true, audiences

00:02:30.571 --> 00:02:35.860
may be more discerning now about these kinds
of troublesome tropes and recycled plot points.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.280
High expectations

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.460
Even though it's not a sure-thing that Avatar
2 will succeed, Cameron isn't just planning

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.100
to bring us one Avatar sequel — he's working
on four of them, with release dates slated

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.820
through 2025.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.620
These films are expected to be shot back-to-back
with a combined budget of $1 billion, which

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.750
is an enormous gamble, not only from a financial
but also from a storytelling standpoint.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.819
Cameron's silenced the doubters of his massive
ambition before, of course.

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:04.660
But he's going to have to not only prove he's
got enough material to justify making four

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.460
full sequels, but that he can earn enough
return on those pictures to compensate for

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.030
the insane amount of front-end investment.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:15.680
Considering no other film has ever come close
to toppling Avatar's box office supremacy,

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.150
even the most optimistic studio executive
has to know Avatar 2 might not live up to

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.010
the gains of its predecessor.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.340
One wrong move, and it could spell disaster
for Lighthouse Pictures and Fox.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.260
That's a lot of pressure for the filmmaker
to be under, so it's a wonder why he didn't

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.320
just opt to make one follow-up movie first
and measure the audience's interest before

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.130
plunging head-first into such an expensive
undertaking.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.290
Trounced tech

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.730
When Avatar first arrived in theaters, it
was one of a kind.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.150
Sure, audiences had seen 3D movies in theaters
before, but none had ever quite had the kind

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.030
of majestic color schemes and landscapes that
this movie offered.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.180
Since then, though, such visual effects have
become much more commonplace in blockbuster

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.000
films, from the superhero sphere to the world
of animated films.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.030
Though James Cameron has insisted that his
Avatar sequels are going to boast some similar

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:05.541
technological breakthroughs to keep audiences
intrigued, it may be tough for Avatar 2 to

00:04:05.541 --> 00:04:07.530
find new ground to break in that regard.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.380
Rumor has it, he may even be working on a
way to deliver the experience without the

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.000
use of glasses, but will it be enough to get
people to the ticket booth?

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.120
It doesn't bode well for him that audiences
have grown weary of seeing movies in 3D.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.810
In recent years, ticket sales for 3D screenings
have dropped like a rock—from about 20%

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.100
of ticket sales in 2010 to 14% in 2016.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:32.840
Even IMAX theaters have drastically reduced
their 3D slate, citing the "clear preference"

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.490
of consumers for 2D.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:40.720
Simply put: the groundbreaking CGI and industry-leading
3D that drove Avatar's must-see status in

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.710
2009 may be virtual non-factors in 2020, which
means Avatar 2 will have to stand on its own

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:47.940
merits.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.400
Quiet cast

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.780
Apart from Sigourney Weaver, the cast of Avatar
was made up of largely lesser-known actors

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.170
who did a fine enough job, but haven't gone
on to top the A-list.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:01.699
Zoe Saldana is an exception to that rule,
as she's earned prime placement in other space

00:05:01.699 --> 00:05:06.080
stories like Star Trek and Guardians of the
Galaxy since then, but the rest of the cast

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.870
has remained relatively unknown.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.470
So, the fact that most of the original stars
signed on for the sequels didn't exactly blow

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.090
anyone away.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.470
In fact, it may be problematic that Sam Worthington
is coming back to lead all four films as Marine

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.630
Jake Sully, since the actor has pretty much
failed to break out in any meaningful way

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.830
after his stint in the blue suit.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.900
Since starring in Avatar, Worthington has
put together a string of forgettable roles,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.900
including two ill-advised big-budget reboots
and a bunch of movies that very few filmgoers

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.900
have even heard of.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.480
He's hardly a box office draw.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.810
Of course, he was even less so when the first
film came out, but back then Cameron had the

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.400
benefit of being able to draw everyone in
with his magic filmmaking.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.110
The biggest new name to join the fold is Kate
Winslet, and even she's been on a career downslope

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.110
lately.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.949
So, we'll have to see whether sticking with
actors who don't all command a crowd will

00:05:52.949 --> 00:05:56.150
work out in his favor again this time around.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.680
Stiff competition

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.870
Disney may have a horse in the Avatar box
office race before the second film hits theaters.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:06.500
Not only are they licensed purveyors of Pandora-themed
merchandise, but they've also recently launched

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.090
a Pandora theme world at Orlando's Animal
Kingdom.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:13.449
In addition, they're in the process of purchasing
the vast majority of the film and television

00:06:13.449 --> 00:06:18.169
assets of Avatar's distributor, Fox, which
would make them even greater beneficiaries

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:20.260
of the film's theoretical success.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:25.000
If the deal goes through as expected, Disney
may try to avoid directly competing with the

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.000
sequels.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.150
Until then, though, the House of Mouse doesn't
have to stand down against a long-overdue

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:33.750
sequel to a one-time blockbuster that might
not even reach its intended audience.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:38.280
After all, they're already expected to bring
forth new Star Wars entries through 2025,

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.680
the year in which Avatar 5 is scheduled to
hit screens—and Marvel has a road map for

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.169
their cinematic universe lined up through
2028, and possibly beyond.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:51.370
This kind of predictable competition didn't
exist during Avatar's box office homerun,

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:56.270
so in order for the sequels to succeed, Cameron
will have to meet, and possibly even exceed,

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.509
the storytelling and cinematic appeal of these
well-oiled machine franchises.

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:01.580
Too far

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:06.180
Originally, only a single sequel to Avatar
got the greenlight from the studio.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:10.620
But then Cameron started envisioning the story
as a trilogy, but before long the creative

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.040
process seemed to get completely out of hand.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.520
Speaking with Variety, Cameron issued perhaps
the most ominous sign yet with regard to the

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.520
sequels' quality:

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.430
"The Avatar story arc was originally meant
to be a trilogy, but I overwrite, and my writers

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.430
overwrote as well.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:27.550
[...] Basically the first of the sequels cloned
itself and became two films, so now it's four

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.550
films.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:29.770
And the studio's very happy with it.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.229
They have an opportunity to make more money,
but it's also an opportunity to spend a lot

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:35.759
more money, too, so there's a clench factor."

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:40.199
And that, in a nutshell, is why Avatar 2 and
its sequels should raise some serious red

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:44.610
flags: they're big-budget gambles, a return
to a world which has taken little hold in

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:48.979
the public imagination, with a questionable
story stretched across far more films than

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:50.419
should be needed to tell it.

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:54.330
But, hey, if Peter Jackson could stretch The
Hobbit into three feature-length films that

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.139
made a billion apiece, well, what's to say
James Cameron doesn't defy all odds again

00:07:58.139 --> 00:07:59.139
as well.

00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:00.330
Thanks for watching!

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.009
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:05.080
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

